[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "ADR-2608150200"
  :adr/title "shinshi-video-actor: residency belongs to the machine that holds the GPU, not to the Bots surface"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-15"
  :adr/supersedes ""
  :adr/superseded-by ""
  :adr/body
  "## Context

The owner asked to make shinshi.club's catalog video generation resident —
phrased as *「itonami.cloud の bots の常駐化」* — and approved placing the
publish write-key on gad.

Before this, the generation existed as a Claude skill
(`.claude/skills/shinshi-catalog-video`) plus three scripts in the superproject,
driven by hand. **The launchd job the skill describes as ticking every 30
minutes was never installed**: `com.gftd.shinshi-catalog-video` is absent from
`launchctl list`, and `~/.gftd/shinshi-catalog-video/loop.ledger.edn` contained
only entries written by hand on 2026-08-15. So there was no residency to move —
there was a documented residency that did not exist.

## Decision

Create `cloud-itonami/shinshi-video-actor`, the second service cloud-itonami
operates for club-shinshi (sibling of `shinshi-growth-actor`), and run it as a
**systemd timer on gad** — the machine that already holds the ComfyUI this
generation drives.

Registered in west as `cloud-itonami-shinshi-video-actor` at
`orgs/cloud-itonami/shinshi-video-actor`, pin `96288001`, by single-entry API
commit `e216c3bc` (4177 -> 4178 entries, no other pin touched).

### Why not a Bot in cloud-itonami-app

That surface is deliberately this laptop. `bots.clj` says so in its own words:
*'What a Bot's own computer is here — Not a cloud VM … A Bot's computer is
therefore this machine.'* That is the right answer for a Bot reading mail, and
the wrong one here: the work happens on a GPU box and on Cloudflare, and the
laptop only issues instructions. Under ADR-2608111721's three questions, the
answer to *'what stops if this machine sleeps tonight'* should be **nothing**.

The Bots surface remains the right place to *look at* and *steer* the actor.
This ADR separates where it is watched from where it lives, which is the same
split ADR-2608111721 draws: 常駐の機構は murakumo、常駐する権利は cloud-itonami.

### Why gad and not asher

asher is the only host declared in `probe.cljs`'s `operator-hosts` — the
implementation of `:slot/attested`. Measured 2026-08-15, **asher has no
outbound HTTPS and cannot reach gad's ComfyUI**: `api.cloudflare.com`,
`shinshi.club` and `100.82.98.110:8188` all returned 000. It cannot publish and
it cannot generate.

gad answered `shinshi.club:200` and `api.cloudflare.com:400` (reachable),
already carries `nbb`, `node` and `wrangler`, runs the ComfyUI locally, and has
been up 29 days under systemd. It is **not in `scripts/fleet-ci/nodes.edn`**, so
no gate code sent from a repository is ever scheduled onto it — which is the
substance of fleet invariant 3, not merely its letter.

**gad already holds a write key**: the Radicle seed COB signing identity
(`gates.edn` `:rad`, passphrase item `radicle-seed-gad-passphrase`). So this
does not create a second attested host — but it does surface that gad has been
attested in substance while only asher was attested in declaration.
`operator-hosts` should name gad. Not doing so is safe today only because gad is
absent from `nodes.edn`, which `probe.cljs`'s own docstring calls out as luck
rather than design.

## The actor

`generation ⊣ CatalogGovernor ⊣ append-only ledger`, driven by a durable outer
loop rather than a StateGraph — the same reasoning ADR-2607123000 §3 gives for
the seven sibling `gftd-*-actor` asset actors: a generation is minutes-scale and
the process may die inside one, so recovery is running the tick again.

Two structural choices worth recording:

**Measurement and judgement are separate namespaces.** `measure` runs ffprobe
and returns numbers or `:unanswered true`; `governor` is pure and consumes that.
A gate that fuses them must map 'could not measure' onto pass or fail, and in
this workspace that mapping has always eventually become pass (ADR-2608136000).
Here an unanswered measurement is simply a value the governor rejects.

**The IP containment is upstream of the governor, and it is load-bearing.**
While building `catalog/series-of`, a version that read only the slug suffix
classified `tifa-final-fantasy` as `Astral Realm` — `%-fantasy` matches
`-final-fantasy` — and put Square Enix's Tifa at the head of the candidate
queue. **The governor does not catch this**: the candidate arrives already
carrying an original series name. Caught by running the selector against live
D1 rather than by reading it. The profile's declared series now wins and a
declared non-original series is a refusal rather than a fallback; both
directions are in `test/run.cljs`, which also asserts that the governor's and
the selector's copies of the 12 series are the same set.

## Verified on gad

- 27-case test suite: ALL OK (governor accept + one reject per check, plus the
  `series-of` regression cases)
- `measure` on a real clip: same bytes/frames/geometry as the laptop
- `install.cljs` refuses correctly (exit 2) with the env file absent
- ffmpeg/ffprobe installed (`7:6.1.1-3ubuntu5`); gad had neither

**Measurements are not comparable across hosts.** The same clip reads
140.945 luma / 9.508 motion under macOS ffmpeg 7.x and 141.641 / 9.563 under
gad's 6.1.1. Thresholds are 8 and 1, so no verdict changes — but a stored
number must not be diffed against a fresh one and read as drift.

## Not done — the timer is not enabled

The actor needs a Cloudflare credential on gad for **both** halves of its work:
the candidate scan reads D1, and the publish writes it. The blob upload does not
— it goes through `shinshi.club/b/upload` with a purpose-built token, which is
the right shape.

The laptop's wrangler uses an **OAuth session** in
`~/Library/Preferences/.wrangler`, which must not be copied to a GPU box. The
only account credential in the secrets map is `CLOUDFLARE_GLOBAL_API_KEY` —
account-wide, unscopeable, and worse to place on gad than the OAuth session; it
was also not retrievable from kagi in this session (`no such item`, which per
that map is not proof of absence). The keychain `gftd.cf` token is DNS-scoped
and returns `Authentication error` against the D1 API, measured.

**Remaining owner step:** issue a Cloudflare API token scoped to D1 edit on
`ai-gftd-shinshi` and `ai-gftd-pds-recordlog`, write it plus
`SHINSHI_UPLOAD_TOKEN` into `/etc/shinshi-video-actor.env` (0600) on gad, then
`nbb deploy/install.cljs --apply`. Until then the install script refuses, which
is the correct behaviour: a timer firing without credentials would burn a GPU
slot per tick and hold every clip it made.

A better shape than a Cloudflare API token, deferred: give the appview a
token-gated record-write route beside `/b/upload`, so no Cloudflare account
credential leaves the laptop at all. That is a change to a live shared Worker
and was out of scope here.

## Consequences

- The catalog fill stops depending on the laptop being awake, once the
  credential lands.
- One more machine holds a publish credential. It is the machine that already
  held a signing key and already cannot receive gate work.
- The 12 original series are now asserted identical in two places by test. IP
  characters are excluded twice, independently, and the outer exclusion is the
  one that actually matters.
- `operator-hosts` in `probe.cljs` should gain gad. Filed as follow-up, not
  done here: changing gate rotation membership mid-session without re-running
  probe is exactly the ordering hazard that docstring warns about."}]
